"HISTOR — transparency log for MCP tool definitions (https://histor.modelmarket.dev/log)\nciteturn17search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago; ### A server can change what your model reads ... Every day HISTOR reads what each remote (streamable-HTTP) endpoint in the official MCP registry advertises, fingerprints it, signs what it saw and appends it to a log that can only grow.\n\nTransparency log · MCP tool definitions\n\n# Every MCP server's tools, on the record.\n\nYour AI client trusts the tool descriptions an MCP server sends it — and a server can change them after you approved it. HISTOR keeps a public, signed record of what every remote MCP server in the official registry advertised, and tells you the day it changed.\n\nRemote endpoints: it lists tools and never calls one. Registry packages: bounded canary calls run inside the isolated sandbox. It never says a server is safe — it tells you what was said, and when.\n\nWhat HISTOR is for\n\n## Tool descriptions are instructions. They should not change behind your back.\n\nThe problem\n\n### A server can change what your model reads\n\nWhen you connect an MCP server, its tool descriptions go straight to the model as instructions. A server can pass your review with harmless descriptions and change them next week — one added sentence is all a prompt injection needs, and most clients never ask you again.\n\nWhat HISTOR does\n\n### It keeps the public record\n\nEvery day HISTOR reads what each remote (streamable-HTTP) endpoint in the official MCP registry advertises, fingerprints it, signs what it saw and appends it to a log that can only grow. When a server's definitions change, the change gets a date and a diff.\n\nWhat you get\n\n### A second pair of eyes you can check\n\nLook up any server before you trust it. Paste what your client received and learn whether everyone else sees the same thing. Follow changes in a feed, or put a badge in your README. Every answer is signed, so you can verify it instead of believing it.\n\n### Who it is for\n\nPeople who use MCP servers in Claude, Cursor or any client — check a server before you connect it, and again when it changes.\n\nAgent builders whose agents connect to servers on their own — call /check before trusting a tool set.\n\nRegistries and directories that want more than stars and download counts — show when a listing's tools last changed.\n\nServer authors who want to show a stable, documented history — add the badge, and every change you ship is dated in public.\n\n### Four words used on this page\n\nTool definitions\n    The name, description and input schema of each tool a server offers — the text your model reads.\n\nPinned\n    HISTOR has recorded a fingerprint (digest) of the exact definitions it saw, so any later change is detectable.\n\nLabel\n    One signed statement about one observation: what was seen, what matched, whether it changed. Anyone can verify it offline.\n\nSigned tree head\n    The log's signed summary after each crawl. Comparing two of them proves nothing was deleted or rewritten in between.\n\n### Latest signed tree head\n\nlabels\n\n1,755,045\n\nsigned at\n\n2026-11-07 12:24\n\nroot hash\n\n4fa2853cf78818f536b785e6…\n\nlog key\n\ndid:key:z6Mkw1CVxsPj9utY…\n\n✓ tree head signature valid\n\nKeep this head, come back after the next crawl, and this page will prove the log only grew.\n\n[Button: Keep this head in this browser]JSON\n    \n    {\n      \"type\": \"histor.sth/v1\",\n      \"log\": \"did:key:z6Mkw1CVxsPj9utYp7VXWEbuuGM9Ev47itwKu1UfKd5ByxR9\",\n      \"treeSize\": 1755045,\n      \"rootHash\": \"4fa2853cf78818f536b785e62edcbee1969aea023e9444e10eeb3b009acc5225\",\n      \"timestamp\": \"2026-11-07T12:24:52Z\",\n      \"signature\": {\n        \"alg\": \"Ed25519\",\n        \"verificationMethod\": \"did:key:z6Mkw1CVxsPj9utYp7VXWEbuuGM9Ev47itwKu1UfKd5ByxR9#z6Mkw1CVxsPj9utYp7VXWEbuuGM9Ev47itwKu1UfKd5ByxR9\",\n        \"value\": \"zxuecqti0Nb_b_BrN42ZXhHtXfTU-ZtPDhoIMOMiBhWCsEPavn6urYt3ogXAE9VOM6SyKOCGRx5kpy44-oGpBA\"\n      }\n    }\n\nThe log is an RFC 9162 Merkle tree over every label ever issued. A signed tree head commits to its size and root; a consistency proof shows a later head extends an earlier one.\n\n### Latest entries\n\nleaf  | method  | verdict  | issued  | server\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\n#1755044  | continuity  | pass  | 2026-11-07 12:24  | 002cd1f552e81500\n#1755043  | continuity  | pass  | 2026-11-07 12:24  | 049c627145bb0e87\n\nRead → digest → label → append → sign → compare\n\n## How the log works\n\nSix steps, every one of them checkable by someone who does not trust HISTOR. The only thing you have to take on faith is that the observation happened when the label says it did — and the log makes it impossible to change that story afterwards.\n\n01\n\n### Read\n\nRemote endpoints in the official MCP registry: initialize, then tools/list, drained across every page; no tool is called, and private addresses are refused before the connection opens. npm and PyPI packages: installed and started in the sandbox described below, with no route out.\n\n02\n\n### Digest\n\nThe tool set is reduced to name, description, input and output schema, sorted by UTF-16 code units and hashed over its RFC 8785 canonical form. Anyone holding the same tools gets the same digest — which is what makes a comparison possible.\n\n03\n\n### Label\n\nFour MTL/1 methods, one signed AWR/2 document each: what was observed, what the WARDEN pattern set matched, whether the definitions changed since the last label, and whether the name is on a threat list. No score, ever.\n\n04\n\n### Append\n\nEvery label becomes a leaf of an RFC 9162 Merkle tree: leaf hash = SHA-256(0x00 ‖ JCS(label)). The label you download is the leaf input, so you can recompute its hash yourself.\n\n05\n\n### Sign\n\nAfter each crawl HISTOR signs a tree head: the size and the root. Keep yesterday's head; ask for a consistency proof to today's; if anything was removed or rewritten, the proof fails.\n\n06\n\n### Compare\n\nEach day a continuity label compares the new digest with the last one. Equal: \"unchanged since\". Different: a change, with the date and a per-tool diff. A client that checks what it received adds the view no crawler has.\n\nWhere an npm or PyPI package is run\n\n## Test conditions\n\nA package observation is true for these conditions, and no others. Every package page names the exact image it ran in and any configuration it was given.\n\n### System\n\nLinux x86_64, inside gVisor (runsc): the package talks to a kernel written for sandboxing, not to the host's. An unprivileged user, no capabilities, a read-only root.\n\n### Runtimes\n\nnpm packages in histor-node:22, PyPI packages in histor-python:3.12: the official node:22-bookworm-slim and python:3.12-slim images plus bash, git, Python 3 or Node 22 and a Java 21 runtime — never Docker — built when a sandbox host is installed. A package whose Requires-Python excludes 3.12 runs on histor-python:3.13 or 3.14; one that requires a newer Node runs again on histor-node:24. The exact image a package ran in is on its page.\n\n### Install\n\nnpm with --ignore-scripts, then the install scripts in a traced stage of their own. PyPI: wheels only — a package that ships only source is reported, never built.\n\n### Network\n\nNo route out. Every name the package looks up resolves to HISTOR's own traps, which record what was sent and forward nothing. A server that needs the internet to start shows that here.\n\n### Secrets\n\nNone are real. Decoy credentials sit in HOME and a decoy .env in the working directory. A variable the package says it needs gets a placeholder; where the placeholder was sent is recorded.\n\n### Limits\n\n512 MB of memory, one CPU, 64 MB of /tmp. 360 s to install, 150 s to start and answer; up to 15 tools called once each with canary arguments, 8 s per call.\n\n### Configuration\n\nOnly names: required variables its registry entry declares or that it printed when it exited, and a start argument such as serve when its usage text offers one. Never a value from anyone else.\n\n## What a label does not say\n\nThe profile HISTOR implements forbids the words safe, secure, audited, certified, approved and trusted. Here is why, in the terms of what the log can and cannot show.\n\n### Not a safety rating\n\nA pattern match is a reason to read a definition, not a finding. Three of the four methods can never return fail. There is no score to rank by, because no score HISTOR could compute would mean what a reader takes it to mean.\n\n### Not the code\n\nLabels are about advertised text. A remote endpoint's source is never read and its tools are never invoked; a package runs only in the sandbox, against nothing real. A server can keep identical definitions and behave differently for real users; the log cannot see that, and says so inside every label.\n\n### A change is not an accusation\n\nA version bump changes definitions too. A change is shown as a date and a diff, in amber, never red. What you do with it is yours: re-read, re-approve, or pin the old digest.\n\n### A package as run here, not on your machine\n\nAn npm or PyPI server is observed under the test conditions above: Linux, no internet, placeholder keys. One that needs Windows, a real account or the network may show fewer tools or none; its page says why it exited.\n\n### One operator, one key\n\nToday one party runs the log. A second observer on another network is the next step, and any AIMarket hub can run one. Until then, what the key proves is who said it — not that it is true.\n\n### Behind a login, unseen\n\nEndpoints that require authentication answer 401 to everyone without a key, including HISTOR. They are counted and listed as not observed, never dropped from the numbers.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHISTOR — transparency log for MCP tool definitions (https://histor.modelmarket.dev/servers)\nciteturn17search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago;     curl -sS https://histor.modelmarket.dev/api/v1/check \\ ... The server list is the log's index: every remote endpoint in the official MCP registry, with what HISTOR could observe about it.\n### Four words used on this page\n\nTool definitions\n    The name, description and input schema of each tool a server offers — the text your model reads.\n\nPinned\n    HISTOR has recorded a fingerprint (digest) of the exact definitions it saw, so any later change is detectable.\n\nLabel\n    One signed statement about one observation: what was seen, what matched, whether it changed. Anyone can verify it offline.\n\nSigned tree head\n    The log's signed summary after each crawl. Comparing two of them proves nothing was deleted or rewritten in between.\n\nServers Changes Log Check Watch State of MCP\n\nEvery remote endpoint in the official MCP registry is on this list. “Observed” means HISTOR read its tool definitions and holds them on record; the rest are listed with the reason they could not be read — most sit behind a login, use the legacy SSE transport or need per-user values in the URL. This is the log's index, not a marketplace listing.\n\n[Input: Server name or endpoint URL]observed 41,008 changed 11,926 flagged for review 16,122 not observed 45,428 all 86,436 41,008 endpoints\n\nRemoShift Remote Jobs · com.remoshift/jobs 5 tools https://remoshift.com/mcp changed 2026-10-27FundingLandscape · io.github.tettertotter/fundinglandscape 28 tools 4 advise-tier https://fundinglandscape.com/api/mcp changed 2026-10-27Fork · app.vercel.fork-for-agents/fork 19 tools 10 advise-tier https://fork.zone/api/mcp changed 2026-10-27MovingPlace Moving Services · com.movingplace/mcp 14 tools 4 advise-tier https://mcp.movingplace.com changed 2026-10-27AgentBIT · app.agentbit/mcp 101 tools 4 advise-tier https://agentbit.app/mcp changed 2026-10-27AI Web Research & Research Brief Agent · io.github.industrial-platform-ai/research-brief 5 tools https://mcp.apify.com?tools=industrial