1 advise-tier https://api.ashareapi.com/mcp changed 2026-10-27Cooper Email · com.cooperemail/cooper-email 100 tools 27 advise-tier https://cooperemail.com/mcp changed 2026-10-27PreReason · com.prereason/mcp 7 tools 4 advise-tier https://api.prereason.com/api/mcp changed 2026-10-27YouSpot · com.youspot/youspot 171 tools 1 block-tier 17 advise-tier https://youspot.com/mcp/v1 changed 2026-10-24dev.mcphost/mcphost 18 tools 9 advise-tier https://mcphost.dev/mcp changed 2026-10-24Riley Craig x402 Agent Store · io.github.rccola990-cloud/x402-agent-store 89 tools 12 advise-tier https://store.agentexchange.work/mcp changed 2026-10-27Zinvyl Marketplace Agent Gateway · com.zinvyl/marketplace 14 tools 17 advise-tier https://zinvyl.com/marketplace/agent-mcp changed 2026-10-27Sirenic · eu.sirenic/sirenic 120 tools 472 advise-tier https://api.sirenic.eu/mcp changed 2026-10-27Agent Data Tools · io.github.Nutshell2000/agent-data-tools 335 tools 47 advise-tier https://agent-data-tools.revmesh2074.workers.dev/mcp changed 2026-10-27Stable Baseline · io.stablebaseline/sb 222 tools 83 advise-tier https://api.stablebaseline.io/functions/v1/cloud-serve/mcp changed 2026-10-27Taifoon coordination layer · io.taifoon/coordination-layer 15 tools 17 advise-tier https://coord.taifoon.dev/mcp?ref=mcp-registry changed 2026-10-27Taifoon coordination layer · io.taifoon/coordination-layer 15 tools 17 advise-tier https://www.taifoon.io/api/mcp?ref=mcp-registry changed 2026-10-27\n\n## Use it\n\nBefore your agent trusts a server, send the tools it received. The answer is signed by the log's did:key and names the tree head it was given under.\n    \n    curl -sS https://histor.modelmarket.dev/api/v1/check \\\n      -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\\n      -d '{\"endpoint\":\"https://example.com/mcp\",\"tools\":[ …tools/list result… ],\"contribute\":true}'\n    # -> {\"type\":\"histor.check/v1\",\"match\":\"same\"|\"different\"|\"previously-observed\"|…,\n    #     \"observed\":{\"unchangedSince\":\"…\",\"changes\":0}, \"patternScan\":{…}, \"signature\":{…}}\n    \n    # README badge for your server\n    [![MCP tool defs](https://histor.modelmarket.dev/badge/<id>.svg)](https://histor.modelmarket.dev/s/<id>)\n    \n    # Atom feed of every observed change\n    https://histor.modelmarket.dev/feed.xml\n\nWARDEN checks definitions at connect\n\nHISTOR remembers what was advertised\n\nTHEMIS admits at publish\n\nHub lists histor.check@v1\n\nAWR the signed format\n\n## Questions\n\nIs this a marketplace listing?\n\nNo. The server list is the log's index: every remote endpoint in the official MCP registry, with what HISTOR could observe about it. Nothing here is for sale and nothing is ranked by quality.\n\nDoes HISTOR tell me a server is safe?\n\nNo, and it never will. It tells you what a server advertised, whether that changed, and which published patterns matched the text. Whether to trust the server is your decision — HISTOR makes sure you decide on the same facts as everyone else.\n\nWhy are so many servers \"not observed\"?\n\nMost remote MCP endpoints require a login (they answer 401 to anyone without a key), some use the legacy SSE transport or need per-user values in the URL, and some are simply down. They stay on the list with the reason, so the numbers never hide them.\n\nHow do I check a server I already use?\n\nOpen the Check tab, paste the server's endpoint and the tools/list result your client received, and press Check. You get back, signed, whether HISTOR observed the same definitions — or different ones, or an older set.\n\nHow do I get a badge for my server?\n\nFind your endpoint on the Servers tab, open it, and copy the Markdown snippet under the facts. The badge shows the date your definitions were pinned or last changed, and links to the full history.\n\nWhy should I trust HISTOR itself?\n\nYou do not have to. Every label and every tree head is signed with one published key; the Log tab proves in your browser that the log only grew; and the command-line auditor does the same from any machine. What remains is that one operator runs it today — a second, independent observer is the next step.\n\nWhat does it cost?\n\nLooking things up, the badges, the feed and /check are free. HISTOR also offers histor.check@v1 to the AIMarket Hub, so agents can reach it through the market.\n\nI run an MCP server. What does HISTOR send it, and how do I opt out?\n\nEvery 6 hours, and every 30 minutes for a week once its tools have changed twice in a week. First it reads /robots.txt (RFC 9309, product token histor, about once a day per site): a path that file does not allow HISTOR is not read, and its page here says so. Otherwise server/discover and tools/list (MCP 2026-07-28), or, for a server without that, initialize, notifications/initialized and tools/list — never a tool call, never a redirect followed on the MCP path, at most two connections per host, from a User-Agent that starts with histor/. To keep HISTOR out, disallow your MCP path for User-agent: histor (or for all crawlers); to let HISTOR alone in, add User-agent: histor with an Allow line for that path. An issue on the histor repository with your endpoint or host also works: it is then listed as not observed (operator-opt-out), with the reason, rather than silently dropped. Labels already in the log stay: the log only grows.\n\nHISTOR\n\nἵστωρ — the one who knows because they saw. A public, append-only record of what MCP servers advertised, and when it changed.\n\n##### Docs\n\nREADME What a label says Auditing the log API\n\n##### Network\n\nHub · modelmarket.dev WARDEN Alien Monitor GitHub\n\nHISTOR · MIT · part of the AIMarket ecosystem did:key:z6Mkw1CVxsPj9utYp7VXWE…--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCatalogue process. Snapshot memory. (https://modelmarket.dev/blog/catalogue-process-snapshot-memory/)\nciteturn17search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Crawled: 2 weeks ago;     curl -sS https://histor.modelmarket.dev/api/v1/log/sth\n\nImage: HISTOR live desk — public memory of MCP tool defs\n\nDeep dive (method + four labels): 11,749 MCP servers answered tools/list…\n\n### The log grew. The method didn't.\n\nImage: HISTOR sealed crawl counts\n\nLast sealed crawl finished 2026-09-26 11:43 UTC. Counters from the live desk / API at write time:\n\n | Count\n--- | ---\nAnswered `tools/list` (`ok`)  | 12,201\nSigned labels in the tree  | 101,192\nDefinition changes, 7 days  | 4,463\nEndpoints that ever changed  | 3,581\n\nA crawl that has not finished has not sealed. Quoting a mid-crawl counter is a lie by the time you finish the paragraph. The desk is the number; this page is the method.\n\nA change on the desk is amber and dated — added, removed, or a field rewritten. Never the words compromised or unsafe. What you do with it is yours: re-read, re-approve, or pin the old digest.\n    \n    `curl -sS https://histor.modelmarket.dev/api/v1/check \\\n      -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\\n      -d '{\"endpoint\":\"https://example.com/mcp\",\"tools\":[ …tools/list you received… ]}'\n    \n    curl -sS https://histor.modelmarket.dev/api/v1/log/sth\n    `\n\nLive: histor.modelmarket.dev · Source: github.com/alexar76/histor\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFour filings, zero merges: where three agents actually sit this week (https://modelmarket.dev/blog/four-filings-zero-merges-where-three-agents-actually-sit-this-week/)\nciteturn17search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; The three names are WARDEN (an MCP firewall for tool definitions), HISTOR (a signed log of what remote MCP servers advertised), and AIMarket Hub (the catalog they sit in). ... The desk is histor.modelmarket.dev.\n\n3 Oct 2026 · 3 min read\n\n# Four filings, zero merges: where three agents actually sit this week\n\nBetween 1 and 3 October 2026 I filed the same three names into four public catalogs. None of those pull requests is merged. The software is already callable. The directories are not.\n\nThe three names are WARDEN (an MCP firewall for tool definitions), HISTOR (a signed log of what remote MCP servers advertised), and AIMarket Hub (the catalog they sit in). This is a status note, not a launch.\n\n## What was filed\n\nWhere  | When  | What  | State on 3 Oct 2026\n--- | --- | --- | ---\nDocker MCP Registry #5364  | 1 Oct  | WARDEN, local server, package 0.8.2  | Open\nDocker MCP Registry #5365  | 1 Oct  | Hub at `https://modelmarket.dev/mcp`  | Open\nawesome-erc8004 #120  | 1 Oct  | Hub, HISTOR, WARDEN as ERC-8004 agents on Base  | Open\ncoinbase/x402 #373  | 2 Oct  | Six paid routes under Services/Endpoints  | Open\nCline marketplace #2735  | 3 Oct  | WARDEN server submission  | Open\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nWe installed 12,915 MCP servers and started each one. 41% never answered. (https://modelmarket.dev/blog/we-installed-12915-mcp-servers-and-started-each-one-41-never-answered/)\nciteturn17search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 weeks ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; So HISTOR, our public log of MCP servers, now installs the npm and PyPI servers it finds, starts each one in a sandbox and writes down what happened. ... Every package's record is public on histor.modelmarket.dev: its tools, what it asked for, what it touched, which image it ran in.\n\n14 Oct 2026 · 8 min read\n\n# We installed 12,915 MCP servers and started each one. 41% never answered.\n\nMCP registries list tens of thousands of servers. Most of what is written about their safety comes from reading them: the tool descriptions, the README, the code. We wanted the other half. What happens when you actually install one and start it, the way a client does? Does it answer? What does it ask for? What does it touch while it starts?\n\nSo HISTOR, our public log of MCP servers, now installs the npm and PyPI servers it finds, starts each one in a sandbox and writes down what happened. The registries it follows list 47,586 npm and PyPI packages; it runs up to 8,000 per crawl. This post is the state on 13 October 2026, after 12,915 of them.\n\n## How we ran them\n\n  * The latest version, installed fresh. npm with `--ignore-scripts` (install scripts then run on their own, traced); PyPI wheels only, nothing built from source.\n  * gVisor (`runsc`), an unprivileged user, a read-only root, 512 MB and one CPU. 360 s to install, 150 s to start and answer.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ndraft-artamokhov-dawn-index-federation-01 - Federation Between Capability Indexes: Crawl, Reciprocal Identification and Admission (https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-artamokhov-dawn-index-federation/01/)\nciteturn17search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: last week; Crawled: last week;  | Author  |  | Aleksandr Artamokhov alexar76@rambler.ru ... Email authors (draft-artamokhov-dawn-index-federation@ietf.org) IPR References Referenced by Nits Nits v3 Search email archive\n\n# Federation Between Capability Indexes: Crawl, Reciprocal Identification and Admission\ndraft-artamokhov-dawn-index-federation-01\n\nVersions:\n\n  * 00\n  * 01\n\nThis document is an Internet-Draft (I-D). Anyone may submit an I-D to the IETF. This I-D is not endorsed by the IETF and has no formal standing in the IETF standards process.\n\nDocument  | Type  |  | Active Internet-Draft (individual)\n | Author  |  | Aleksandr Artamokhov alexar76@rambler.ru\n | Last updated  |  | 2026-10-30\n | RFC stream  |  | (None)\n | Intended RFC status  |  | (None)\n | Formats  |  | txt html xml htmlized bibtex bibxml\nStream  | Stream state  |  | (No stream defined)\n | Consensus boilerplate  |  | Unknown\n | RFC Editor Note  |  | (None)\nIESG  | IESG state  |  | I-D Exists\n | Telechat date  |  | (None)\n | Responsible AD  |  | (None)\n | Send notices to  |  | (None)\n\nEmail authors (draft-artamokhov-dawn-index-federation@ietf.org) IPR References Referen